  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1214 AM EST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nPleasant fall weather will continue over the next few days as a \nlarge area of surface high pressure remains over the region under \nWNW flow aloft. After chilly early mornings, we will see mild \nafternoons with high temperatures in the 60s for most valley \nlocations each day through Wednesday.   \n\nA change will be arriving for the latter part of the week as models \ncontinue to show a shift to more winter-like conditions.  The \ndetails are still in flux, but models generally show multiple short \nwaves diving southeast out of Canada that dig out a large upper \ntrough/closed low over the eastern CONUS Thursday and Friday. This \nupper low then begins to lift northeast over the weekend. \n\nThe initial short wave and associated cold front will likely bring \nsome rain to the area Wednesday night into Thursday. Lapse rates \nsteepen enough that a few rumbles of thunder may be possible, \nalthough right now convective energy looks very limited.  Colder air \nwill move in behind the front, and the colder air will then be \nreinforced as subsequent short waves rotate around the upper low. \nThe details are still very uncertain this far out and will be \nimportant for our sensible weather, but this set up still looks \nfavorable for some wintry precipitation especially across the higher \nelevations at times in the Thursday night through Saturday time \nframe, with accumulating mountain snowfall possible that will likely \nbe enhanced by upslope flow. Snowflakes may even mix in over \nportions of the valley at some point although that is less certain. \nHigh temperatures may struggle to make it much above the 40s in many \nvalley locations both Friday and Saturday.\n\nSunday looks to be dry as the upper low pulls away, but still on the \ncool side.",
